@startuml
!pragma layout smetana
!include _uml-style.puml
title Cancellation after publication — UML sequence
hide footbox
participant "caller" as Caller
participant "<color:#FFFFFF>native front\n<color:#FFFFFF>(runner inside)" as Front #1E4F78
participant "managed process" as Managed
database "result channel\n(temporary files)" as Channel #FFF1CF

note over Front, Channel
  Selection and launch have succeeded. Worker cleanup was confirmed.
  The managed process holds this session's RAVEL_EXIT_FILE.
end note
Managed -[#135E50]> Channel : Publish "candidate" via end-session
note over Managed, Channel
  First complete publication wins.
  end-session success does not confirm delivery.
end note
== Cancellation reaches the front ==
Managed -> Front : SIGINT (the example below)
Front -> Managed : Attempt to stop the managed process's group
Front -> Front : Attempt reap and terminal recovery if lent;\ncheck whether the group is gone
Front -> Channel : Attempt result channel cleanup
Front -> Front : Attempt remaining required cleanup
note over Front, Channel
  A failed attempt is reported, not treated as confirmed cleanup.
  No X certifies process or directory removal on every path.
end note
Front -> Front : Keep cancellation primary; suppress candidate;\nretain original failure and cleanup reports
Front --> Caller : Report cancellation; re-raise INT\n(shell status 130); no result bytes

legend bottom
  Read downwards: grouped order, not elapsed time. Dashed verticals: lifelines.
  Solid arrows: actions; dashed arrow: reported outcome; bent arrows: local work.
  Blue: Ravel process. Grey: external participants. Gold cylinder: temporary files.
  Green carries a published value, but no green arrow reaches the caller.
  The managed process's group includes descendants that stay in that group.
  INT goes directly to the front here; terminal Ctrl-C can reach only the group.
  HUP and TERM use shell statuses 129 and 143; failed propagation uses numeric fallback.
  Cleanup problems cannot turn cancellation into success or permit result delivery.
endlegend
@enduml
